# view_ply.tgl -- look at a .ply made somewhere else.
#
# Point `file` at any Gaussian-splat .ply in the standard 3DGS layout -- the
# kind SuperSplat, Polycam, Luma, Nerfstudio and the INRIA reference code
# write -- or at a plain point cloud. The 3D viewer draws it like one made
# here: drag to orbit, the "splats" checkbox, the scale slider.
#
# What comes through: position, shape, rotation, opacity and base colour.
# What does not: view-dependent colour (the file's f_rest spherical-harmonic
# terms), which tglab's splats do not have -- so reflections and sheen are
# averaged away. The load report says when a file had them.
#
# Up is whatever the file says it is. Most splat tools use the COLMAP camera
# convention, as tglab does, but a file oriented otherwise will simply look
# rotated; orbit to it.

imported = load_ply( file = "D:/lrwork/TEST/Big Flower pot MK/scene.ply" )

display( imported, "imported" )

# To write it back out -- after trimming or carving, say -- save() takes any
# reconstruction to .ply. The app writes saves when asked, not on every run.
#
#   save( imported, "D:/splats/scene_copy.ply" )
